          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\n  - pattern/Hybrid-Search\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Substantive technical source; addresses production RAG systems. Single source; would benefit from corroborating source.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines **sparse lexical search** (e.g. BM25) with **dense vector search**, then merges the two ranked result sets. The merged results are typically combined via **reciprocal rank fusion**, a ranking aggregation technique that captures both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Why Hybrid?\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on queries mixing:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- Natural language intent\\n\\nEach method has distinct strengths: sparse search excels at exact matches and rare terms; dense search captures semantic relationships and paraphrases.\\n\\n### Production Status\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the **default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems**, reflecting its robustness across diverse query types and domains.\\n\\n### Merging Strategy\\n\\n**Reciprocal rank fusion** is the standard technique for merging ranked lists from sparse and dense retrievers. See [[retrieval-patterns]] for details on this ranking aggregation approach.\\n\u0022}"
        }
